WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.939 --> 00:00:11.300
Hello everyone thank you for coming to the
NCBI webinar, on the evolution of NCBI-Hackathons.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:19.120
Peter Cooper, the head of the communications
group, is here with me to answer specific

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:20.120
questions.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.600
I will answer more general questions at the
end of the webinar.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:30.010
Answers that we don't get to will be available
linked to our webinars page.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.090
Also the materials will be available on our
FTP site.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.719
All right let's go ahead and get started.

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:40.379
At this point, we have run 13 NCBI-Hackathons.

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:48.920
Over the course of the webinar, that tells
you about what they are, what we do in these

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.699
NCBI style hackathons, and how you can get
involved.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:01:00.050
What we do in NCBI style hackathons in short,
we put together teams of 5 to 6 individuals

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:06.080
that work on bioinformatics projects in a
collaborative way, and 80 percent of those

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.400
teams finishes a functional software proto
type at the end of three days,.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.470
These are not three 24-hour days, in fact
typically they are three nine-hour days.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.140
Those nin hours are really filled with very
focused work.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.180
When we started these hackathons, we were
teaching a lot of workshops in next generation

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:32.900
sequencing and we thought hackathons were
a good way to offer advanced next generation

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.450
sequencing courses.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:40.370
What we realized is what we were doing is
we were rapidly building software prototypes,

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:46.500
ant that was the major motivations for people
coming to these Hackathons, so what we ended

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:53.810
up doing was optimizing the Hackathons such
that the teams develope software prototypes.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:02:01.870
Like I sais earlier, about 80 percent of teams
develope working software prototypes.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:08.240
In our first hackathon we built four functional
software products.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.150
Including things like variant calling for
RNA-Seq.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:17.290
That was neat, and did a survey of available
tools for variant calling for RNA-Seq.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:24.300
Those tools have progressed a lot, but it
was nice to have a place to see things in

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.880
one place.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:31.480
In August 2015 we built six functional software
products, and a couple of them including the

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:37.480
sort of maching learning Pharmacogenomics_Prediction_Pipeline,
are still in active development.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.260
In fact for the Hackathons in total, we've
developed about 80 software products, and

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.860
about 40 are stable or still in active development.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.490
This is a picture, a subset of the people
that were in that particular Hackathon, and

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:57.900
this is approximately what an NCBI-Hackathon
looks like, although as I will discuss later

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.790
they are now in many different locations.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:07.099
All the tools that we build in NCBI hackathons
are totally open source.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.470
We put everything on github.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:16.420
As I'll talk about that later, we also containerize
things so we also see manifestations, on docker

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:20.900
hub, jupyter and other places as well.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:26.760
We build several things in NCBI Hackathons,
and things end up in several places.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:33.440
Some things end up being worked in to NCBI
production, I'd say that's a minority of things.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:41.930
Other things end up being part of our standalone
bioinformatics tools, but other things really

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:47.090
end up and being a part of the educational
experiences and we work them into the webinars.

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:52.730
And also into larger scale online genomic
courses.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:56.460
One thing that I am particularly proud of,
we built an on-line educational resource for

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:04.180
RNA-seq which is self-guided you can go to
it right now and fire up some cloud instances

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.120
either on Google cloud engine or Amazon Web
Services, and teach yourself how to do RNA-seq

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:15.870
mapping with STAR, HISAT and magic-BLAST.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.500
Like I said, we worked this into an online
workshop.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.480
The first five lectures are available on YouTube
right now.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:30.600
In 2016 we extended the frequency, these hackathons
have become really popular and we started

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.660
having them in other places, including Cold
Spring Harbor at ISMB.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:43.030
And also hack-seq was an NCBI style hackathon
that was run in Vancouver and I was a participant

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:49.070
not an organizer of that event and it was
a very special and very large NCBI style Hackathon.

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:56.150
So then in January we went back to our main
Hackathon rotation.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:02.760
We really started building things that integrated
with larger software pipelines, including

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:07.380
things like SRA2R, which you can see here
in the middle of yours screen, which is an

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:15.850
R package that will probably end up in Bioconductor
as a way to port data from SRA into R frameworks.

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:19.890
We also started partnering with other groups.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:21.320
I mentioned hackseq before.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.320
We were also able to start building novel
things.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:30.310
Like for example, ways to compare structural
variants to structural variant databases.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:37.240
And that's been a neat focus of some of our
work, working with structural variants, as

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:42.570
in my opinion there are not enough tools to
analyze that kind of data in the Boinformatics

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:43.570
community.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:49.880
Like I said, we started working with bioconda,
with linux brew and with Bioconductor.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:55.840
A lot of the things in our Hackathons, deal
well with the sequence read archive, like

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.430
the SRA2R package that I mentioned earlier.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:02.729
We are also interested in fostering comminity
involvement with these hackathons and I'll

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:05.210
talk about that even more at the end.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:12.250
But we were able to build a platform for metadata
categorization for NLM indexers.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:19.730
So they were able annotate data in GEO, which
has been a bioinformatics community hobby

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.210
for the last 15 to 20 years.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:30.259
They actually annotated all of the metadata
for 14,000 Drosophila RNA-Seq datasets, which

00:06:30.259 --> 00:06:33.060
we then used in a collaboration with FlyBase.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:43.030
We have also been able to harmonize some of
the metadata in DbGaP, with the NIH Common

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.360
Data Elements repository.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:51.150
Another thing I am excited about is the ability
to do really experimental things.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:58.100
To our knowledge up to this point it was very
difficult for people to put full genomes into

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.430
a graph, few people have attempted it.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:08.270
So in a hackathon we ran in Cold Spring Harbor
we were able to load seven full human genomes,

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:11.570
into a VGE phased graph structure.

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:15.949
That was something I think was quite neat,
and a project that is ongoing.

00:07:15.949 --> 00:07:21.949
We have also been able to work quite a bit
on immunogenic peptides, looking at classifying

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:28.330
different types of immunogenic peptides from
cancer, bacteria, as well as viruses.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:32.880
This is something that I think is of particular
clinical relevance, this is called DangerTrack.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.590
This effectively is a public blacklist for
regions of the human genome that are relatively

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:42.210
unstable and really unsuitable for algorithmic
short variant calling.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:48.380
This basically tells you where as a clinician
you may want to double check before you report

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:53.300
a particular variant for a patient.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:58.830
I have a question from the audience, asking
if these hackathons are focused solely on

00:07:58.830 --> 00:07:59.830
genomics.

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:00.830
This is a good question.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:06.210
The hackathons are mostly genomics-based,
but we have done some with some non-genomic-based

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:10.199
projects in some external hackathons.

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:14.770
In fact we had one project that was working
one with EEG technology.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.759
That said, the majority are based around genomics.

00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:26.319
But with the big caveat that each January
we have a biomedical informatics hackathon

00:08:26.319 --> 00:08:28.270
at the National Library of Medicine.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:33.539
And that really gets into a lot of things
involving text mining and so on.

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:35.380
Thank you for the question.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:43.180
In 2017 we have really evolved to emphasize
containerization, publication and accessibility

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:46.290
with these Hackathon products.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:52.490
And I'm really sort of proud of this because
I think one of the things the hackathons contribute

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.940
to the community is speeding up science.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:00.610
I told you before that at this point, about
80% of the Hackathon products become functional

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:05.160
prototypes, but another thing I'm particularly
proud of is that about 10% of the get published

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:11.670
as manuscripts often within a couple of months
of finishing the product in the Hackathon.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.690
That to me is really quite neat.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:18.490
I think accelerating publicaiton in my opinion
is very important.

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:22.920
One of the things that is very accessible
that we built in a hackathon is Phenvar.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.930
And you can see the URL here is phenvar.colorado.edu.

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:34.970
This will generate word clouds of concepts
co-mentioned with SNPs in PubMed abstracts.

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:39.440
But my friends like to remind me that word
clouds are not really serious science, but

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:46.810
what is serious science is that is will also
generate D3 rendered networks of SNPs and

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:49.240
in particular PMIDs.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.320
That may be useful to some of the folks in
the audience.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:10:00.140
And to the question before, this is an example
of some of the text mining stuff we do and

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.270
integrating this with genomics.

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:04.580
Something that is sort of pure text mining.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:05.750
We built something called PubRunner.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.140
I'm very excited about PubRunner.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:14.190
Bascially what it is, this is a framework
for narutral processing tools that continuously

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:17.010
updates the PubMed corpus.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:23.040
This framework can also be used to update
other corpora for natural language process

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:28.570
research, and we are working on packaging
this and collaborating with a lot of other

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:32.580
groups, to allow them to auto update their
PubMed corpus.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.160
If you are interested in this, please send
me an email.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.830
It's ben, b-e-n dot b-u-s-b-y @nih.gov.

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:45.260
The head developer of this in the Hackathon,
Jake Lever is currently working in the group

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:50.070
for the next four weeks on this project, so
this would be a particularly good time to

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.140
contact me.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:58.390
We have also been able to take other people's
software products, and really put them into

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.190
more reproducible frameworks.

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:03.990
We have a common workflow language pipeline
for epigenomics called SCREW.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:11.019
And if you do epigenomcs, I'd really encourage
you to check this out, because I think that

00:11:11.019 --> 00:11:15.050
reproducibility is really important, particularly
in the epigenomics space.

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:20.680
We have been able to collaborate, in this
case with GA4GH.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:27.650
This is a slide generated by the global alliance
data working group.

00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:31.560
It shows NCBI as a repository that works with
their API.

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:37.880
At the time they made the slide, NCBI's data
repositories actually did not work with their

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:39.510
reads API.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:43.839
But in a Hackathon, we were able to stitch
these two things together, and it really worked

00:11:43.839 --> 00:11:45.500
quite beautifully.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:51.610
And right now we are working to extend this
to be able to work with raw data on the fly.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:56.890
So the idea is the reads API can work even
on unaligned data.

00:11:56.890 --> 00:12:03.240
That is something I am particularly excited
about as we go to more and more decentralized

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.710
databases for this type of genomic data.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:16.540
In other things, we have done things like
looking at resources for RNA-seq, both viewing

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:21.300
and doing counts, as well as identification
of novel viruses.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.230
This is something I'm particularly excited
about.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:30.940
But also when we announce teams that want
to work on virus identification and integration,

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.690
they are extremely popular at the Hackathon's.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:41.610
So I think this is a topic that really grabs
the imagination and enthusiasm of folks that

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.220
attend our hackathons.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:47.750
Speaking of, I want to take a brief segue
and talk about the kind of people that come

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:49.560
to our hackathons.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:54.529
Typically they are computational biologicals
who are either senior graduates or postdocs.

00:12:54.529 --> 00:12:59.730
And people who lead teams at hackathons are
typically senior postdocs or people at the

00:12:59.730 --> 00:13:01.970
assistant professor level.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:06.100
There are exceptions to every rule we have
had fairly young people attend and even lead

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.430
teams at hackathons.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:15.170
If you are interested please contact me directly
or simply sign up for a Hackathon.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:21.610
We have been able to foster community involvement
with the Hackathon's projects in a couple

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:22.610
of other ways.

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:29.170
We are starting to build educational resources
that help people leverage community tools.

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:33.220
And I will talk about an expansion of this
later.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:39.430
This is the EDirect Cookbook, which will be
mentioned in next week's NCBI webinar on API's.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:45.670
But, I'll show you that these are jupyter
notebooks that we are building to enhance

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:46.670
NCBI education.

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:55.950
We are hoping to build a whole suite of computational
resources that are used to leverage NCBI databases.

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:58.800
We think that this will be helpful to the
community.

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.430
If you are interested in getting involved
in those projects whether through Hackathons

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:09.720
or through other means, please fill free to
contact myself or Peter Cooper.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:14.490
Next I want to show you some cutting edge
stuff that we built just a couple of weeks

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:17.329
ago, these are projects I am very excited
about.

00:14:17.329 --> 00:14:23.830
I'm proud to say they are still fairly stable
and in ongoing development.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:30.200
One of the things that we built is a simple,
very easy to use tool for looking at antimicrobial

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:33.300
resistance genes in metagenomic data sets.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:39.170
This heavily leverages Magic-BLAST, which
is a new flavor of BLAST optimized to deal

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:45.709
with next generation sequencing reads that
we built at NCBI, and also uses the CARD database

00:14:45.709 --> 00:14:48.980
to identify some of these metagenomic resistance
genes.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:55.639
Although I think it is attractive to integrate
databases as well as put in some of learning

00:14:55.639 --> 00:14:57.560
modules.

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:02.070
I encourage you to go to github, grab some
code.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.070
Check it out.

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:08.800
It's docklerized and very easy to leverage
and use on anybody's linux system.

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:11.300
Right now the cancer genomics cloud is free.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.570
You can also leverage it there.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:15.300
That may be something to check out.

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:20.690
We are also interested in streaming data.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:28.580
What this particular thing does, we built
a database of adapters, that are present in

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:31.500
the SRA.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:36.660
If you know what the adapters are, in SRA,
for the particular dataset, then you can trim

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:39.130
them on the fly.

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:44.850
If you want to do assembly, and generate contigs,
now you do not have to do fastq-dump anymore,

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:49.620
you can simply trim your adaptors on the fly,
and then build contigs from streaming data,

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:56.210
which is something I think is very attractive
to the NCBI infrastructure; also, with genomic

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:57.779
data getting larger.

00:15:57.779 --> 00:16:02.790
If you have any general questions on that
I would love to answer them; you can email

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.160
me directly.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:10.150
I was particularly proud of a team, coming
from and a particular academic institution,

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:15.600
and they wanted to build a viewer for an excellent
mouse aging dataset.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:25.589
What they ended up doing was building modulular
types of viewers for existing RNA-seq viewers.

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:31.279
I think that is important because there are
currently 35 RNA-seq viewers that are up in

00:16:31.279 --> 00:16:34.190
the community and supported; about 35.

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:40.160
I think it is really important to say, if
you have a particular thing you want to be

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:44.079
able to view, it is great to build modular
tools that you can insert into existing viewers.

00:16:44.079 --> 00:16:48.639
This is something that we have become interested
in.

00:16:48.639 --> 00:16:54.730
At the end of the Hackathon, we are able to
generate movies looking at mouse aging with

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:57.420
and without infections to flu.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:03.610
And what is really amazing to me, Even at
this extremely zoomed out level of the mouse

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:12.199
genome, you are able to see responses to influenza,
over long periods of time.

00:17:12.199 --> 00:17:17.909
There is a question about virtual Hackathons
but I will wait until the end to get into

00:17:17.909 --> 00:17:18.909
that.

00:17:18.909 --> 00:17:21.689
This was a really experimental project.

00:17:21.689 --> 00:17:29.979
I look forward to iterating on this project,
to be frank, this team was filled with brilliant

00:17:29.979 --> 00:17:36.210
people, but I'm not sure random flipping is
the most prudent obfuscation model.

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:41.809
What this does is send a robot into anybody's
data set, looks for particular variants, then

00:17:41.809 --> 00:17:46.769
if it is desired by the people who own the
data, it obfuscates the data so it is no longer

00:17:46.769 --> 00:17:49.509
personally identifiable.

00:17:49.509 --> 00:17:55.549
That switch can be flipped back off to generate
a full data matrix if some collaboration agreement

00:17:55.549 --> 00:17:58.159
is reached.

00:17:58.159 --> 00:18:02.070
Obviously consistent with the patient consents
on the data.

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:06.309
We have also been helping out with other people's
Hackathon's.

00:18:06.309 --> 00:18:08.070
That's something I'm particularly proud of.

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:13.659
We have helped out with Bio-IT, as well as
recently, an artificial intelligence genomics

00:18:13.659 --> 00:18:15.330
hackathon in San Fransisco.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:20.299
And these are things we look forward to working
with.

00:18:20.299 --> 00:18:25.899
Also on the NCBI Hackathons github site, which
I'll put up on the last slide, you can find

00:18:25.899 --> 00:18:34.659
a repo for being able to run your own NCBI
style Hackathons, which I would be happy to

00:18:34.659 --> 00:18:36.320
help out with as well.

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:41.519
But it you just want to roll out your own
please feel free to take any pieces of this

00:18:41.519 --> 00:18:44.289
and do that.

00:18:44.289 --> 00:18:51.309
We would like to see more of this, there is
no shortage of folks interested.

00:18:51.309 --> 00:18:55.240
Our next hackathon is in Pittsburg PA at the
end of September.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:19:03.399
We will likely work on an out-of-the-box
automatic Hackathon builder.

00:19:03.399 --> 00:19:06.940
I am pretty excited about that.

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:13.889
I mentioned publications out of the hackathon,
and one of the places we publish these hackathon

00:19:13.889 --> 00:19:20.809
results, and allow others to publish these
results is in F1000 research and they have

00:19:20.809 --> 00:19:25.940
been wonderful in working with us in publishing
Hackathon tools.

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:31.359
But certainly some communication of these
tools has gone to other journals as well.

00:19:31.359 --> 00:19:38.149
You can also follow @DCgenomics on twitter,
and other places on twitter we try to advertise

00:19:38.149 --> 00:19:39.619
things in these hackathon.

00:19:39.619 --> 00:19:46.779
This is a picture of some of the folks in
the most recent New York Genome Center Hackathon,

00:19:46.779 --> 00:19:47.960
in June.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:51.309
It was a fantastic experience.

00:19:51.309 --> 00:19:55.960
We had a lot of folks from that metro area
as well as folks that came in from across

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.600
the country and other countries.

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:01.870
Like I said here is a list of some of the
upcoming Hackathons.

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:07.529
If any of you are in California, there will
almost certainly be a Northern California

00:20:07.529 --> 00:20:13.730
hackathon the first week in April, likely
at either UCSC or UCSF.

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:19.539
We're trying to work on a Southern California
Hackathon that may occur in January.

00:20:19.539 --> 00:20:22.640
Please check this site for that.

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:35.619
Finally you can find the actual repos both
at this github organization, and stable and

00:20:35.619 --> 00:20:44.519
other projects that are continuing to be developed
can be found at this URL, ncbi-hackatons.github.io.

00:20:44.519 --> 00:20:46.109
Thank you very much for your time.

00:20:46.109 --> 00:20:53.299
We have blog posts coming out on NCBI Insights
about some of the products from the last three

00:20:53.299 --> 00:20:58.059
hackathons; Boulder, CO, NY Genome Center,
as well as the one we just ran at NCBI.

00:20:58.059 --> 00:21:00.759
Now I'm going to take questions.

00:21:00.759 --> 00:21:02.679
I actually have one queued up here.

00:21:02.679 --> 00:21:07.109
This is an excellent question.

00:21:07.109 --> 00:21:10.669
Are there plans to host virtual Hackathons?

00:21:10.669 --> 00:21:14.899
We have done quite a few experiments with
virtual hackathons.

00:21:14.899 --> 00:21:23.269
One thing we have seen is that when we involve
virtual people, we actually see not even sort

00:21:23.269 --> 00:21:30.789
of less production, but dramatically less
production from the people that are involved

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:32.100
online.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:33.590
That has been our experience so far.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:37.470
However, the last experiment we did on that,
we had Hackathon veteran.

00:21:37.470 --> 00:21:44.450
Someone who had been to several hackathons
working part-time to work on modular tools,

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.769
and that was a very positive experience.

00:21:46.769 --> 00:21:53.279
We've also had people who did quite a bit
of work done online after the hackathons.

00:21:53.279 --> 00:22:01.710
What I can see a model of is Hackathon veterans,
or people coming to in person Hackathons of

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:06.320
this style, and then continuing to work on
them independently.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:12.859
That said, of course there are other models
that work well online, sort of long-term competitive

00:22:12.859 --> 00:22:15.690
hackathons tend to work very well online.

00:22:15.690 --> 00:22:20.989
One of the things we've thought about doing
is in the virus discovery space, as well as

00:22:20.989 --> 00:22:30.150
antimicrobial resistance space, is hosting
long-term online Hackathons to leverage some

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:36.340
of the tools we have built in the in person
hackathons and use them to analyze data sets.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:41.700
I would also like to mention there will be
a second year of hackseq.

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:47.059
That's the big, awesome genomics hackathon
in Vancouver I mentioned.

00:22:47.059 --> 00:22:50.549
You can go there and register at hackseq.com.

00:22:50.549 --> 00:22:57.740
A neat thing is that now there are other hackathons
running on this model.

00:22:57.740 --> 00:23:03.049
Someone else asked, does the hackathon have
a Slack channel or team?

00:23:03.049 --> 00:23:06.529
In fact this is a great question.

00:23:06.529 --> 00:23:14.169
Each hackathon has it's own Slack organization,
which obviously persist after the hackathons.

00:23:14.169 --> 00:23:19.039
We originally ran them through Google groups
and than through Slack and that's been very

00:23:19.039 --> 00:23:20.039
popular.

00:23:20.039 --> 00:23:25.840
We also have a linkedIn in group for hackathon
alumni, where jobs are posted, people can

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:30.539
talk about issues, that sort of thing, and
we also make announcements.

00:23:30.539 --> 00:23:36.099
That's one thing I am very proud of, what
I think we are doing with the NCBI Hackathons,

00:23:36.099 --> 00:23:42.849
and other NCBI style hackathons like hackeq,
is we are creating a community of excellent

00:23:42.849 --> 00:23:50.779
computational biologists, but are also seeing
evidence to suggest these people work well

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:52.509
with other people.

00:23:52.509 --> 00:23:56.909
And really in my experience, they are really
kind and nice people.

00:23:56.909 --> 00:24:04.070
I think this actually makes an excellent tool
for building a phenomenal computational biology

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:05.169
workforce.

00:24:05.169 --> 00:24:13.690
That alumni group may expand from being a
mostly linkedIn presence, to Slack and other

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:15.200
avenues of social media.

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:23.179
I would like to mention in the third week
of June 2018, we are having Hackathon in Boulder

00:24:23.179 --> 00:24:28.943
Colorado, as a part of a genomics conference,
and we would like that to be what we are calling,

00:24:28.943 --> 00:24:31.849
mostly a champions Hackathon.

00:24:31.849 --> 00:24:38.080
So, mostly hackathon alumni that come to Boulder
and work to integrate some of the projects,

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:43.379
and to build larger scale bioinformatics platforms.

00:24:43.379 --> 00:24:50.840
Bringing those people back and having them
continue to work for the bioinformatics community.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:55.340
Finally we are starting to integrate with
other bioinformatics hackathons that you may

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:57.120
be familiar with.

00:24:57.120 --> 00:25:02.840
I have been to the BOSC Hackathons and we're
talking with them about integrating, as well

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:07.210
as the general biohackathons community, and
elixir.

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:12.859
Those are things I look forward to telling
you about sometime soon.

00:25:12.859 --> 00:25:14.129
Great.

00:25:14.129 --> 00:25:17.999
Thank you for the really great questions,
people who have asked questions.

00:25:17.999 --> 00:25:25.429
With that, typically many times I don't put
the Q&amp;A session on the recording, but I think

00:25:25.429 --> 00:25:27.759
perhaps for this one it will be popular.

00:25:27.759 --> 00:25:30.469
Thank you to everyone who attended.

00:25:30.469 --> 00:25:35.799
All of you Hackathon alumni out there; there's
a couple in attendance here.

00:25:35.799 --> 00:25:38.950
With that we will sign off.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:42.570
If you have any further questions please feel
free to email me.

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:48.769
We just have one or two spots available for
the Pittsburgh Hackathon.

00:25:48.769 --> 00:25:57.769
The deadline is closed, but if you are able
to go to Pittsburgh September 25, 26 and 27,

00:25:57.769 --> 00:25:59.009
please go ahead and sign up.

00:25:59.009 --> 00:26:05.190
Thank you very much everybody have a fantastic
rest of your day and a fantastic week tune

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:13.600
in next week for an NCBI webinar on NCBI APIs,
you will find there are a lot more NCBI APIs

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:15.100
you thought there were.

